---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CascadeDiagram from '../../components/CascadeDiagram.astro';
import Sidenote from '../../components/Sidenote.astro';

// Split-comparison figure: 28 items per row, ~30% test.
const N = 28;
const randomTest = new Set([2, 5, 6, 11, 14, 19, 20, 25]);
const cells = Array.from({ length: N }, (_, i) => i);
const sq = 18;
const gap = 6;
const x0 = 24;
const cellX = (i: number) => x0 + i * (sq + gap);

// Accuracy dot plot: map value → x on a 0.70–0.85 axis.
const ax = (v: number) => 240 + ((v - 0.7) / 0.15) * 440;
const rows = [
  { y: 45, label: 'DistilBERT · noisy labels', v: 0.731, c: 'ink' },
  { y: 80, label: 'DistilBERT · cleaned labels', v: 0.753, c: 'c0' },
  { y: 115, label: 'Gemini few-shot', v: 0.81, c: 'c1' },
  { y: 150, label: 'Cascade (deployed)', v: 0.81, c: 'c0' },
];
const ticks = [0.7, 0.75, 0.8, 0.85];
---

<Base
  title="News classification by confidence-gated cascade — Shangwei Yang"
  description="Classifying TaiwanPlus news videos into nine categories: finding data leakage, cleaning labels with an LLM, and routing by confidence to halve API cost."
>
  <article class="prose has-margin">
    <p class="eyebrow">Case study · 2026 · TaiwanPlus</p>
    <h1>News classification by confidence-gated cascade</h1>
    <p class="meta">
      PyTorch · Hugging Face Transformers · Gemini API · scikit-learn ·
      <a href="https://github.com/shangweiyang79/TaiwanPlus-News-Classification">github repo</a>
    </p>

    <p class="lede">
      TaiwanPlus needed its YouTube news videos sorted into nine editorial categories<Sidenote
        id="cats"
      >
        International Relations, US Relations, China Relations, Military, Politics, Social &amp;
        Rights, Business &amp; Economy, Environment &amp; Climate, Culture &amp; Lifestyle.
      </Sidenote> using only what every video is guaranteed to have: a title and tags. The final system
      reads 0.81 accuracy at roughly half the API cost of a pure-LLM approach — but the numbers are
      the least interesting part. This is a story about the three times my evaluation lied to me.
    </p>

    <h2>1 · The split was lying</h2>
    <p>
      My first fine-tuned DistilBERT scored 0.81 on a random train/test split. Too good. News
      arrives in bursts — one developing story yields a week of near-duplicate titles with the same
      label — and a random split scatters each burst across both sides. The model wasn't
      classifying; it was recognizing siblings it had memorized.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 230" role="img" aria-label="Two rows of squares comparing a random split, where test items are scattered among training items, with a chronological split, where all test items come after the training items in time.">
        <text class="d-label" x={x0} y="28">random split</text>
        {
          cells.map((i) =>
            randomTest.has(i) ? (
              <rect class="cell-test" x={cellX(i)} y="38" width={sq} height={sq} />
            ) : (
              <rect class="cell-train" x={cellX(i)} y="38" width={sq} height={sq} />
            )
          )
        }
        <path class="d-line-c1" d={`M ${cellX(4)} 66 L ${cellX(4)} 72 L ${cellX(6) + sq} 72 L ${cellX(6) + sq} 66`} />
        <text class="d-label-sm" x={cellX(4)} y="88">same story, both sides of the split</text>

        <text class="d-label" x={x0} y="128">chronological split</text>
        {
          cells.map((i) =>
            i >= 20 ? (
              <rect class="cell-test" x={cellX(i)} y="138" width={sq} height={sq} />
            ) : (
              <rect class="cell-train" x={cellX(i)} y="138" width={sq} height={sq} />
            )
          )
        }
        <path class="d-line d-dashed" d={`M ${cellX(20) - gap / 2} 132 L ${cellX(20) - gap / 2} 162`} />
        <path class="d-line" d={`M ${x0} 176 L ${cellX(27) + sq} 176`} />
        <path class="d-line" d={`M ${cellX(27) + sq - 6} 171 L ${cellX(27) + sq} 176 L ${cellX(27) + sq - 6} 181`} />
        <text class="d-label-sm" x={x0} y="196">time →</text>
        <text class="d-label-sm" x={cellX(27) + sq} y="196" text-anchor="end">train on the past · test on the future</text>

        <rect class="cell-train" x="530" y="14" width="14" height="14" />
        <text class="d-label-sm" x="550" y="26">train</text>
        <rect class="cell-test" x="610" y="14" width="14" height="14" />
        <text class="d-label-sm" x="630" y="26">test</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-no">Fig. 1</span>Random splits leak near-duplicate stories into the test
        set. Rebuilding the splits chronologically — the shape of actual deployment — dropped
        accuracy from 0.81 to an honest ~0.75.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>2 · The labels were lying</h2>
    <p>
      With honest evaluation in place, tuning bought almost nothing. Reading the misclassified rows
      did: many "errors" were the model being right and the label being wrong. The source labels
      came from operational metadata that was never designed as training ground truth.<Sidenote
        id="audit"
      >
        Audit mechanics: Gemini re-classified every training row against the same nine-category
        schema; disagreements were flagged and re-examined, corrections fed back into training.
      </Sidenote> An LLM-assisted label audit lifted DistilBERT from 0.731 to 0.753 — more than any
      hyperparameter sweep.
    </p>

    <h2>3 · The benchmark was lying</h2>
    <p>
      Scored against the noisy source labels, Gemini few-shot looked mediocre. Scored against a
      human-verified test set, it reached 0.81 and beat my fine-tuned model — the old benchmark had
      been punishing it for disagreeing with wrong answers. Once the ranking was trustworthy, the
      design question changed from <em>which model</em> to <em>which model, when</em>.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 210" role="img" aria-label="Dot plot of accuracy on the human-verified test set: DistilBERT with noisy labels 0.731, DistilBERT with cleaned labels 0.753, Gemini few-shot 0.81, and the deployed cascade also 0.81.">
        {ticks.map((t) => (
          <>
            <path class="grid-v" d={`M ${ax(t)} 25 L ${ax(t)} 168`} />
            <text class="d-label-sm" x={ax(t)} y="188" text-anchor="middle">{t.toFixed(2)}</text>
          </>
        ))}
        {rows.map((r) => (
          <>
            <text class="d-label-sm" x="228" y={r.y + 4} text-anchor="end">{r.label}</text>
            <path class="grid-h" d={`M 240 ${r.y} L ${ax(r.v)} ${r.y}`} />
            <circle class={`dot-${r.c}`} cx={ax(r.v)} cy={r.y} r="5" />
            <text class="d-label" x={ax(r.v) + 12} y={r.y + 4}>{r.v.toFixed(3).replace(/0+$/, '').replace(/\.$/, '')}</text>
          </>
        ))}
        <text class="d-label-sm" x="680" y="208" text-anchor="end">accuracy · human-verified test set · n = 1,179</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-no">Fig. 2</span>Every point on this chart is the same task; only the
        honesty of the measurement improves left to right. The cascade matches pure-Gemini accuracy
        while sending it only the hard 40% of traffic.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>The cascade</h2>
    <p>
      DistilBERT is free to run; Gemini is accurate but metered. The production system lets the
      local model keep any prediction it makes with softmax confidence ≥ 0.80 (about 60% of
      traffic) and escalates the rest.<Sidenote id="tau">
        τ = 0.80 was chosen on the validation set as the point where DistilBERT's accepted
        predictions match Gemini's accuracy — below it, you pay for answers the free model already
        gets right.
      </Sidenote> The result matches pure-Gemini accuracy at roughly half the API cost.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <CascadeDiagram />
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-no">Fig. 3</span>Deployed routing. Residual errors concentrate in the
        geopolitical categories (International / US / China / Military), where the ambiguity is
        genuine — human annotators disagree there too.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>What this project demonstrates</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Evaluation before optimization</strong> — leakage found, split rebuilt, labels audited, then and only then tuned.</li>
      <li><strong>Cost as a design constraint</strong> — the interesting engineering is matching top accuracy at half the price, not squeezing another half-point.</li>
      <li><strong>LLMs as instruments, not oracles</strong> — Gemini serves as annotator, benchmark referee, and escalation tier, each with its output checked.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="meta">
      Code, scripts, and the training notebook:
      <a href="https://github.com/shangweiyang79/TaiwanPlus-News-Classification">
        shangweiyang79/TaiwanPlus-News-Classification</a
      >. Sample data is synthetic; the production dataset is proprietary.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.12rem;
  }
  .cell-train {
    fill: var(--grid-soft);
    stroke: var(--grid);
  }
  .cell-test {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--c1);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
  }
  .grid-v {
    stroke: var(--grid-soft);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }
  .grid-h {
    stroke: var(--grid-soft);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }
  .dot-ink {
    fill: var(--muted);
  }
  .dot-c0 {
    fill: var(--c0);
  }
  .dot-c1 {
    fill: var(--c1);
  }
</style>
